WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:08.020
In the last video, you saw the loss function and the cost function for logistic regression.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:13.740
In this video, you'll see a slightly simpler way to write out the loss and cost functions

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:18.860
so that the implementation can be a bit simpler when we get to gradient descent for fitting

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.860
the parameters of a logistic regression model.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.660
Let's take a look.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:29.220
As a reminder, here's the loss function that we had defined in the previous video for

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.740
logistic regression.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:37.220
Now, because we're still working on the binary classification problem, y is either 0 or 1.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:45.620
Because y is either 0 or 1 and cannot take on any value other than 0 or 1,

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:50.420
we'll be able to come up with a simpler way to write this loss function.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.540
You can write the loss function as follows.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:59.780
Given the prediction f of x and the target label y, the loss equals

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:10.180
negative y times log of f minus 1 minus y times log of 1 minus f.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:15.020
And it turns out this equation, which we just wrote in one line,

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:20.540
is completely equivalent to this more complex formula up here.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:22.100
Let's see why this is the case.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:30.220
Now, remember, y can only take on the values of either 1 or 0.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:36.060
In the first case, let's say y equals 1.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:43.940
This first y over here is 1, and this 1 minus y is 1 minus 1,

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.380
which is therefore equal to 0.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:54.980
And so the loss becomes negative 1 times log of f of x minus 0 times a bunch of stuff.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:58.580
That becomes 0 and goes away.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:04.300
And so when y is equal to 1, the loss is indeed the first term on top,

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:08.180
negative log of f of x.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:14.340
Let's look at the second case when y is equal to 0.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.620
In this case, this y here is equal to 0.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:21.700
So this first term goes away.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:29.930
And the second term is 1 minus 0 times that logarithmic term.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:38.850
So the loss becomes this negative 1 times log of 1 minus f of x.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:44.450
And that's just equal to this second term up here.

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:47.810
And so in the case of y equals 0,

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:53.210
we also get back the original loss function as defined above.

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:57.970
So what you see is that whether y is 1 or 0,

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:03.410
the single expression here is equivalent to the more complex expression up here,

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:08.370
which is why this gives us a simpler way to write the loss with just one equation

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:13.970
without separating out these two cases like we did on top.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:16.570
Using this simplified loss function,

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:22.390
let's go back and write out the cost function for logistic regression.

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:27.030
So here again is the simplified loss function.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:31.430
And recall that the cost J is just the average loss,

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:36.110
average across the entire training set of m examples.

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:43.150
So it's 1 over m times the sum of the loss from i equals 1 to m.

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:46.990
Now, if you plug in the definition for the simplified loss from above,

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:53.350
then it looks like this, 1 over m times the sum of this term above.

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:57.470
And if you bring the negative signs and move them outside,

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.750
then you end up with this expression over here.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:03.230
And this is the cost function,

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:08.870
the cost function that pretty much everyone uses to train logistic regression.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:13.470
Now, you might be wondering, why did we choose this peculiar function

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:18.110
when there could be tons of other cost functions we could have chosen?

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:22.630
Although we won't have time to go into great detail on this in this class,

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:26.150
I'd just like to mention that this peculiar cost function

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:31.110
is derived from statistics using a statistical principle called

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:33.670
maximum likelihood estimation,

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:38.310
which is an idea from statistics on how to efficiently find parameters

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.510
for different models.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:46.670
And this cost function has the nice property that it is convex.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:50.550
But don't worry about learning the details of maximum likelihood.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:53.630
It's plus a deeper rationale and justification

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:57.950
behind this particular cost function.

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:00.710
The upcoming optional lab will show you

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:04.830
how the logistic cost function is implemented in code.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:07.230
I recommend taking a look at it

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:13.870
because you implement this later in the practice lab at the end of the week.

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:16.830
This upcoming optional lab also shows you

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.550
how two different choices of the parameters

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:22.550
will lead to different cost calculations.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:25.110
So you can see in the plot

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:28.270
that the better fitting blue decision boundary

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:34.070
has a lower cost relative to the magenta decision boundary.

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:36.630
So with the simplified cost function,

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:41.670
we're now ready to jump into applying gradient descent to logistic regression.

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:44.750
Let's go see that in the next video.
